1
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:26,327
Imagine a hall in Gaza, a long time ago, on a feast day. Three thousand people are
on the roof alone. The man being led through the noise by a boy cannot see.

2
00:00:26,377 --> 00:00:31,790
He has just been stood where the whole room can see him, and he says something to
the boy.

3
00:00:32,840 --> 00:00:37,670
[Samson] Lead me where I can feel the pillars supporting the temple, so I can lean
against them.

4
00:00:39,220 --> 00:00:48,724
Where I can feel the pillars. Three thousand people are looking at him and he is
asking to be put where his hands can reach something.

5
00:00:48,774 --> 00:00:54,690
Here is Brian, by way of Mister Scaredy Cat, on what a blind man hears in that.

6
00:00:56,740 --> 00:01:03,093
[Brian] That is the most ordinary sentence in the whole chapter, and every blind
person has said a version of it. Put my hand on it.

7
00:01:03,143 --> 00:01:06,570
[Brian] Not point at it, not describe it, put my hand on it.

8
00:01:06,620 --> 00:01:13,760
[Brian] When I am led into a strange room the first thing I want is something to
touch, a wall, a table edge, the back of a chair,

9
00:01:13,810 --> 00:01:17,591
[Brian] because until I have that I am standing in the middle of nothing.

10
00:01:17,641 --> 00:01:24,065
[Brian] The boy leading Samson would have heard a tired man asking for something to
lean on, and Samson let him think so.

11
00:01:24,115 --> 00:01:32,050
[Brian] What his hands were feeling for was the one thing in the building that held
the rest of it up. The room was watching him. He was reading it.

12
00:01:33,600 --> 00:01:43,718
This is Not By Sight, a series on blindness in the Bible, made by a blind Christian
for blind believers, their families, and the church.

13
00:01:43,768 --> 00:01:52,152
The Scripture you will hear is the Berean Standard Bible, read word for word. The
thoughts marked as Brian's are Brian Clark's own.

14
00:01:52,202 --> 00:01:55,070
And when we imagine a scene, we will tell you so.

15
00:01:56,620 --> 00:02:07,781
This episode is part of our series on sight lost and not given back: six people in
the Bible who lost their sight and lived the rest of their lives without it.

16
00:02:07,831 --> 00:02:23,393
We tell them in three pairs. Two men blinded by their enemies, Zedekiah and Samson.
A father and a son, Isaac and Jacob. Two old men of Shiloh, Eli and Ahijah.

17
00:02:23,443 --> 00:02:29,076
Every episode begins with sounds, and with what a blind man hears in them.

18
00:02:29,126 --> 00:02:42,082
Along the way we follow the Hebrew words the Bible uses for eyes that fail, one word
handing on to the next. When we imagine a scene, we will tell you so.

19
00:02:42,132 --> 00:02:49,450
Everything else is from the text, from the old books read in their own voice, and
from Brian.

20
00:02:52,000 --> 00:03:01,526
First, where we are. We began this series with the last king of Judah, blinded by
his enemies and bound in bronze.

21
00:03:01,576 --> 00:03:11,028
We end it with the other man the Bible describes that way, some five hundred years
earlier, in the days when there was no king in Israel at all.

22
00:03:11,078 --> 00:03:21,818
The book of Judges says that again and again, and each time it finishes the sentence
the same way: everyone did what was right in his own eyes.

23
00:03:21,868 --> 00:03:32,645
That is the world Samson was born into. Israel had done evil in the sight of the
LORD, and He had given them into the hand of the Philistines for forty years.

24
00:03:32,695 --> 00:03:41,338
Five Philistine cities held the coast, Gaza the farthest south, and the tribe of Dan
lived in the low hills just above them,

25
00:03:41,388 --> 00:03:45,124
close enough to go down to their towns on a feast day.

26
00:03:45,174 --> 00:03:52,235
In one of those hills, at Zorah, there was a man named Manoah whose wife had no
children.

27
00:03:52,285 --> 00:04:01,563
The angel of the LORD came to her, not to him, and told her she would have a son.
She was to drink no wine while she carried him.

28
00:04:01,613 --> 00:04:13,266
No razor was ever to touch his head, because the boy would be a Nazirite to God from
the womb, set apart, and he would begin to deliver Israel from the Philistines.

29
00:04:13,316 --> 00:04:18,115
Begin, the angel said. Not finish.

30
00:04:18,165 --> 00:04:29,941
She named him Samson, and the text says the boy grew, and the LORD blessed him, and
the Spirit of the LORD began to stir him between Zorah and Eshtaol.

31
00:04:29,991 --> 00:04:36,590
That is the whole of his childhood. The next verse he is a grown man walking down to
a Philistine town.

32
00:04:38,274 --> 00:04:50,178
He went down to Timnah and saw a Philistine woman, and came home and told his
parents to get her for him. They asked whether there was no woman among his own
people.

33
00:04:50,228 --> 00:05:00,023
He answered in the words the whole book of Judges was written around: get her for
me, for she is pleasing to my eyes.

34
00:05:00,073 --> 00:05:10,258
The text adds, in a parenthesis, that his parents did not know this was from the
LORD, who was seeking an occasion against the Philistines.

35
00:05:10,308 --> 00:05:21,757
So his eyes chose, and God used the choosing. On the road to the wedding he tore a
young lion apart with his hands and told no one.

36
00:05:21,807 --> 00:05:29,962
At the wedding feast he set a riddle, and his bride wept it out of him and gave the
answer to her own people,

37
00:05:30,012 --> 00:05:36,285
and he paid the debt with the clothes of thirty men of Ashkelon and went home angry.

38
00:05:36,335 --> 00:05:41,075
When he came back for her, her father had given her to another man.

39
00:05:41,125 --> 00:05:53,202
Samson burned the Philistines' standing grain with three hundred foxes and torches
tied between their tails. The Philistines burned the woman and her father.

40
00:05:53,252 --> 00:06:04,322
He struck them down, and they came up against Judah for him, and a thousand men of
Judah went to the cave where he was and asked him what he had done to them.

41
00:06:04,372 --> 00:06:17,626
He let his own people bind him with two new ropes and hand him over, and at Lehi the
Spirit came on him and the ropes fell off his arms like burnt thread,

42
00:06:17,676 --> 00:06:23,586
and with the fresh jawbone of a donkey he struck down a thousand men.

43
00:06:23,636 --> 00:06:29,226
Then he judged Israel twenty years in the days of the Philistines.

44
00:06:29,276 --> 00:06:40,490
He was the strongest man in the Bible, and from his first word as a man he had a
weakness for Philistine women that his parents saw before he did.

45
00:06:41,540 --> 00:06:50,102
The last chapter of his life has three women in it, and the first is the one in
Gaza, the city we are standing in.

46
00:06:50,152 --> 00:07:00,717
He went in to her at night, and the men of Gaza waited at the gate to kill him in
the morning, and at midnight he got up and pulled the gate out of the wall,

47
00:07:00,767 --> 00:07:05,518
doors and posts and bar, and carried it on his shoulders toward Hebron.

48
00:07:05,568 --> 00:07:13,907
Then came Delilah, in the Valley of Sorek, below his own hills. The text does not
say she was a Philistine.

49
00:07:13,957 --> 00:07:23,837
It says he loved her, and that the lords of the Philistines came to her and offered
eleven hundred shekels of silver each, more than a lifetime's wages,

50
00:07:23,887 --> 00:07:28,802
if she would find out where his strength came from and how he could be tied.

51
00:07:28,852 --> 00:07:38,587
Three times she asked and three times he lied: seven fresh bowstrings, new ropes,
his seven braids woven into a loom.

52
00:07:38,637 --> 00:07:46,310
Three times she called the Philistines in, and three times he walked out. He knew
what she was doing.

53
00:07:46,360 --> 00:07:54,954
The text has her saying so to his face, you have mocked me and lied to me, and he
kept going back.

54
00:07:55,004 --> 00:08:06,537
The fourth time, because she wore him down day after day, he told her the truth. He
had been a Nazirite from the womb; no razor had ever touched his head.

55
00:08:06,587 --> 00:08:15,237
He said it to the one person in the country who was being paid to hear it. And he
fell asleep on her knees. Where this is.

56
00:08:15,287 --> 00:08:23,787
Stand in Gaza, the southernmost of the five Philistine cities, near the sea, and
face north, with north at twelve o'clock.

57
00:08:23,837 --> 00:08:29,183
Ashkelon, another of the five, is half a day up the coast at one o'clock.

58
00:08:29,233 --> 00:08:39,399
The Valley of Sorek, where Delilah lived, and Zorah just above it, where Samson was
born and will be buried, are two days' walk inland at two o'clock.

59
00:08:39,449 --> 00:08:46,694
The valley runs down from the hills below Jerusalem to the coast, and Delilah's
village and Samson's both sat in it.

60
00:08:46,744 --> 00:08:57,091
Jerusalem is at two o'clock too, past Zorah and farther up: three days from Gaza,
and uphill the whole way, from the sea to the hill country.

61
00:08:57,141 --> 00:09:07,289
In Samson's day it was not Israel's city. The Jebusites held it, there was no
temple, and the ark was at Shiloh, where this series spent two episodes.

62
00:09:07,339 --> 00:09:12,458
Five hundred years later the king we began with would be led out of it in bronze.

63
00:09:12,508 --> 00:09:23,490
And at three o'clock, two or three days east and uphill, is Hebron, where he carried
the gate. He is back in Gaza now, and he will not leave it alive.

64
00:09:29,040 --> 00:09:32,750
Judges chapter 16, verses 15 to 22.

65
00:09:33,300 --> 00:09:39,087
[Reader] “How can you say, ‘I love you,’” she asked, “when your heart is not with
me?

66
00:09:39,137 --> 00:09:46,394
[Reader] This is the third time you have mocked me and failed to reveal to me the
source of your great strength!”

67
00:09:46,444 --> 00:09:57,913
[Reader] Finally, after she had pressed him daily with her words and pleaded until
he was sick to death, Samson told her all that was in his heart: “My hair has never
been cut,

68
00:09:57,963 --> 00:10:02,052
[Reader] because I have been a Nazirite to God from my mother’s womb.

69
00:10:02,102 --> 00:10:08,518
[Reader] If I am shaved, my strength will leave me, and I will become as weak as any
other man.”

70
00:10:08,568 --> 00:10:18,357
[Reader] When Delilah realized that he had revealed to her all that was in his
heart, she sent this message to the lords of the Philistines: “Come up once more,

71
00:10:18,407 --> 00:10:21,745
[Reader] for he has revealed to me all that is in his heart.”

72
00:10:21,795 --> 00:10:26,617
[Reader] Then the lords of the Philistines came to her, bringing the money in their
hands.

73
00:10:26,667 --> 00:10:39,212
[Reader] And having lulled him to sleep on her lap, she called a man to shave off
the seven braids of his head. In this way she began to subdue him, and his strength
left him.

74
00:10:39,262 --> 00:10:51,050
[Reader] Then she called out, “Samson, the Philistines are here!” When Samson awoke
from his sleep, he thought, “I will escape as I did before and shake myself free.”

75
00:10:51,100 --> 00:10:55,331
[Reader] But he did not know that the LORD had departed from him.

76
00:10:55,381 --> 00:11:06,831
[Reader] Then the Philistines seized him, gouged out his eyes, and brought him down
to Gaza, where he was bound with bronze shackles and forced to grind grain in the
prison.

77
00:11:06,881 --> 00:11:11,170
[Reader] However, the hair of his head began to grow back after it had been shaved.

78
00:11:12,720 --> 00:11:25,093
He did not know that the LORD had departed from him. That is the saddest sentence in
the book of Judges, and it comes one verse before his eyes.

79
00:11:25,143 --> 00:11:39,863
He woke up the same man he had always been, as far as he knew. Then one verse, and
we will not go past what it says, as we did not for Zedekiah. His eyes were taken.

80
00:11:39,913 --> 00:11:44,971
He was brought down to Gaza, the city whose gates he had carried away.

81
00:11:45,021 --> 00:11:53,138
He was bound in bronze, the same word as the king's shackles, five hundred years
before the king.

82
00:11:53,188 --> 00:12:05,587
And he was put to grinding grain, which is a mill, which is a blind man walking in a
circle. The text does not tell us how long the mill went on.

83
00:12:05,637 --> 00:12:11,230
It tells us one thing happened while it did. His hair began to grow back.

84
00:12:15,280 --> 00:12:25,630
Now imagine the prison, because the text gives the frame and not the days. Bound
with bronze shackles and forced to grind grain.

85
00:12:26,940 --> 00:12:37,012
He knows this room by now the way a blind man knows any room he has worked in. So
many steps round. The place where the floor dips.

86
00:12:37,062 --> 00:12:43,595
The smell of the grain, and of himself. The chain tells him where his feet are.

87
00:12:43,645 --> 00:12:57,716
Above him, through the stone, he can hear Gaza: carts, a market, children, and on
some days, more than the others, a crowd. He learns the sound of a feast day.

88
00:12:57,766 --> 00:13:04,259
He had nine months of nothing but this, or nine years. The text does not say.

89
00:13:04,309 --> 00:13:14,979
What he had no time for at all was the thing most people get when their sight goes:
a last look. He did not know that evening in Sorek that he would want one.

90
00:13:15,029 --> 00:13:23,029
And somewhere in those days, with his hands on the beam of the mill, he would have
felt what the Philistines did not think to check.

91
00:13:23,079 --> 00:13:25,370
His hair was on his shoulders again.

92
00:13:26,420 --> 00:13:28,530
Verses 23 to 31.

93
00:13:29,080 --> 00:13:36,457
[Reader] Now the lords of the Philistines gathered together to offer a great
sacrifice to their god Dagon.

94
00:13:36,507 --> 00:13:44,222
[Reader] They rejoiced and said, “Our god has delivered Samson our enemy into our
hands.”

95
00:13:44,272 --> 00:13:57,027
[Reader] And when the people saw him, they praised their god, saying: “Our god has
delivered into our hands our enemy who destroyed our land and multiplied our dead.”

96
00:13:57,077 --> 00:14:06,827
[Reader] And while their hearts were merry, they said, “Call for Samson to entertain
us.” So they called Samson out of the prison to entertain them.

97
00:14:06,877 --> 00:14:09,668
[Reader] And they stationed him between the pillars.

98
00:14:09,718 --> 00:14:19,116
[Reader] Samson said to the servant who held his hand, “Lead me where I can feel the
pillars supporting the temple, so I can lean against them.”

99
00:14:19,166 --> 00:14:24,782
[Reader] Now the temple was full of men and women; all the lords of the Philistines
were there,

100
00:14:24,832 --> 00:14:30,514
[Reader] and about three thousand men and women were on the roof watching Samson
entertain them.

101
00:14:30,564 --> 00:14:35,857
[Reader] Then Samson called out to the LORD: “O Lord GOD, please remember me.

102
00:14:35,907 --> 00:14:45,074
[Reader] Strengthen me, O God, just once more, so that with one vengeful blow I may
pay back the Philistines for my two eyes.”

103
00:14:45,124 --> 00:14:49,739
[Reader] And Samson reached out for the two central pillars supporting the temple.

104
00:14:49,789 --> 00:14:59,947
[Reader] Bracing himself against them with his right hand on one pillar and his left
hand on the other, Samson said, “Let me die with the Philistines.”

105
00:14:59,997 --> 00:15:10,657
[Reader] Then he pushed with all his might, and the temple fell on the lords and all
the people in it. So in his death he killed more than he had killed in his life.

106
00:15:10,707 --> 00:15:21,467
[Reader] Then Samson’s brothers and his father’s family came down, carried him back,
and buried him between Zorah and Eshtaol in the tomb of his father Manoah.

107
00:15:21,517 --> 00:15:23,990
[Reader] And he had judged Israel twenty years.

108
00:15:27,040 --> 00:15:31,670
Count what the room saw, and what the blind man did.

109
00:15:31,720 --> 00:15:42,525
They saw a shaved, blind, chained man led in by a boy to be laughed at, and they
stationed him between the pillars because that was the middle of the floor,

110
00:15:42,575 --> 00:15:44,264
where everyone could see.

111
00:15:44,314 --> 00:15:56,016
He asked the boy for something to lean on. He put one hand on each pillar. He
prayed, and nobody in the hall heard it; Matthew Henry thinks it was not even out
loud.

112
00:15:56,066 --> 00:16:06,369
And the two things he asked for are the two things the whole chapter had taken from
him. Remember me, from the God who had departed.

113
00:16:06,419 --> 00:16:16,371
Strengthen me, in the arms that had gone weak as any other man's. Once more. He does
not ask for his eyes.

114
00:16:16,421 --> 00:16:30,323
He asks for his strength once, and he says what for, and he does not pretend it is
anything nobler than it is: for my two eyes. God gave it.

115
00:16:30,373 --> 00:16:36,750
And his brothers came down from Zorah, two days at two o'clock, and carried him home
to his father's tomb.

116
00:16:38,300 --> 00:16:39,930
Brian, what do you hear in this one?

117
00:16:40,480 --> 00:16:49,984
[Brian] Samson lost his sight in the prime of his life. So do a lot of blind adults,
and so did I. Most of the people in this series went blind slowly, with age.

118
00:16:50,034 --> 00:16:58,854
[Brian] Samson went blind in a moment, at the height of his strength, and that puts
him closer to me and to the people I work with than any of the others.

119
00:16:58,904 --> 00:17:01,288
[Brian] What I think about first is the adjustment.

120
00:17:01,338 --> 00:17:11,099
[Brian] He was the most physically gifted man in the Bible, and in one afternoon he
was reduced to being led around by a hand, with his strength gone and his eyes gone
with it.

121
00:17:11,149 --> 00:17:18,056
[Brian] The transition was sudden. I had nine months between the diagnosis and the
end of my sight, and that was hard.

122
00:17:18,106 --> 00:17:26,804
[Brian] I work all the time with people who lose their vision in a matter of days or
hours, and they are the hardest for me to work with, because they had no time to
prepare.

123
00:17:26,854 --> 00:17:36,307
[Brian] No time to get their last looks at the things they wanted to remember.
Samson got none. The last thing he saw was Delilah's room, and the Philistines in
it.

124
00:17:36,357 --> 00:17:40,916
[Brian] The Hebrew word matters to me here in a way it may not to a sighted reader.

125
00:17:40,966 --> 00:17:51,409
[Brian] Judges says the Philistines gouged out his eyes, and the verb is naqar,
which the old dictionary defines as to bore, to penetrate, to quarry.

126
00:17:51,459 --> 00:17:55,736
[Brian] Isaiah uses the same word for the quarry a stone is dug from.

127
00:17:55,786 --> 00:18:03,005
[Brian] I have had both of my eyes enucleated, removed, because of the extreme
pressure in them before the last surgeries.

128
00:18:03,055 --> 00:18:09,505
[Brian] My doctor described what he was going to do as scooping out the inside of a
melon. That is naqar.

129
00:18:09,555 --> 00:18:17,114
[Brian] But I want to say plainly that it was one of my better decisions in life. My
eyes had stopped working completely and the pain and discomfort went with them.

130
00:18:17,164 --> 00:18:23,045
[Brian] Samson had no say in it and no anaesthetic, and I know more than I would
like about what was done to him.

131
00:18:23,095 --> 00:18:28,296
[Brian] Then there is purpose, and this is the part of Samson's story that is most
mine.

132
00:18:28,346 --> 00:18:38,677
[Brian] Between Delilah's room and the hall at Gaza there is a mill, and the text
does not say how long he walked in that circle. I know how long mine was. Three
years.

133
00:18:38,727 --> 00:18:45,319
[Brian] I had been an IT manager for sixteen years when my sight went, and when it
went I did not know what I was for anymore.

134
00:18:45,369 --> 00:18:54,960
[Brian] I spent those three years waiting for God to show me what His purpose was
for this stage of my life. I did not get an epiphany. There was no great revelation.

135
00:18:55,010 --> 00:19:03,136
[Brian] What I got was God patiently waiting for me to reach the place where His
purpose could be fulfilled in me. While I waited I did not sit still.

136
00:19:03,186 --> 00:19:08,143
[Brian] I went through blind rehab. I earned a Salesforce administrator
certification.

137
00:19:08,193 --> 00:19:16,551
[Brian] I kept my computer skills sharp, because I suspected that the life I had
already lived would be useful going forward, even if I could not see how.

138
00:19:16,601 --> 00:19:20,457
[Brian] Samson's hair grew while he walked. He did not know what for.

139
00:19:20,507 --> 00:19:27,310
[Brian] I think something was growing in me in those three years too, and I did not
know what for either, until I was asked to put my hand on something.

140
00:19:36,800 --> 00:19:44,511
[Brian] And then the hall. Samson had been the judge and protector of Israel for
twenty years, and blindness did not end that.

141
00:19:44,561 --> 00:19:50,948
[Brian] The text shows him in the temple at Gaza, asking the servant who held his
hand to put him where he could feel the pillars.

142
00:19:50,998 --> 00:19:59,831
[Brian] With nobody left to help him, he asked God for help, and God gave him just
enough strength, once more, to finish what he had been given to do.

143
00:19:59,881 --> 00:20:02,350
[Brian] The book says he did more in his death than in his life.

144
00:20:06,900 --> 00:20:18,085
Now the old books. Matthew Henry, who could see, read this chapter with less pity
and more care than most, and he says the hard thing first.

145
00:20:18,135 --> 00:20:27,197
Samson told Delilah his secret knowing what she was. Three times she had called the
Philistines in; he had watched her do it.

146
00:20:27,247 --> 00:20:37,643
Henry puts it in one sentence that could stand over this whole series: if he had not
been blind before the Philistines put out his eyes,

147
00:20:37,693 --> 00:20:40,408
he might have seen himself betrayed.

148
00:20:40,458 --> 00:20:43,770
His point is that the eyes went last.

149
00:20:43,820 --> 00:20:56,726
The seeing had failed years before, in Timnah, in Gaza, in Sorek, and what happened
in the prison was only the outside of a thing that had already happened inside.

150
00:20:56,776 --> 00:21:07,350
On the prison, Henry notices the hair. It is strange, he says, that the Philistines
did not cut it again, and he supposes two reasons.

151
00:21:07,400 --> 00:21:16,690
Perhaps they wanted his strength back so they could get more work out of him at the
mill. And now that he was blind, they were in no fear of any hurt from him.

152
00:21:16,740 --> 00:21:24,493
They could watch his hair grow back and not care, because they had stopped looking
at him as a man who could do anything.

153
00:21:24,543 --> 00:21:34,913
On the prayer, he supposes it was a mental prayer, not heard of men in that noisy
crowd, yet heard of God and graciously answered.

154
00:21:34,963 --> 00:21:39,170
And he sets the dying judge beside someone else who died praying.

155
00:21:40,220 --> 00:21:48,250
[Matthew Henry] Samson died praying, so did our blessed Saviour; but Samson prayed
for vengeance, Christ for forgiveness.

156
00:21:53,350 --> 00:22:07,444
Henry is right about the difference, and the text does not hide it; Samson says for
my two eyes. But the hair is where Henry lands, and it is where the text lands too.

157
00:22:07,494 --> 00:22:16,748
The growth of it, he says, was neither the cause nor the sign of the return of his
strength, except as the badge of his consecration,

158
00:22:16,798 --> 00:22:20,245
a token that God accepted him as a Nazirite again.

159
00:22:20,295 --> 00:22:28,636
The strength that he had lost by sin, Henry says, he, like a true penitent, recovers
by prayer.

160
00:22:28,686 --> 00:22:40,302
Josephus, writing for Romans, gives Samson a soldier's epitaph instead: he deserves
to be admired for his courage and strength and his greatness at his death;

161
00:22:40,352 --> 00:22:50,270
as for his being ensnared by a woman, that is to be ascribed to human nature; in all
other respects he was one of extraordinary virtue.

162
00:22:50,320 --> 00:22:58,273
Henry calls him a fond fool and asks the reader's pardon for saying so. Both of them
are reading the same man.

163
00:22:58,323 --> 00:23:03,380
The text calls him neither, and only says that he judged Israel twenty years.

164
00:23:04,930 --> 00:23:19,406
Now the last word in the chain, and it is the hardest one. Zedekiah's was "ah-VAR,"
blinded. Isaac's was "kah-HAH," dim. Jacob's was "kah-VED," heavy.

165
00:23:19,456 --> 00:23:39,310
Eli's and Ahijah's was "KOOM," stood still. Samson's is "nah-KAR." To bore. To dig
out. To quarry. It is used six times in the whole Bible, and it is never gentle.

166
00:23:39,360 --> 00:23:49,093
It is the word for the quarry a stone is cut from, in Isaiah. It is the word for
what the ravens do to an eye, in Proverbs.

167
00:23:49,143 --> 00:24:02,072
It is the word Nahash the Ammonite used when he offered the men of Jabesh a treaty
on condition of their right eyes.

168
00:24:02,122 --> 00:24:14,748
It is a stonemason's word, and Judges uses it for a man's face. Brian has told you
why it matters to him. It is the one word in the chain he knows from the inside.

169
00:24:14,798 --> 00:24:23,275
And beside it, in the same verse, is the word that closes the loop. Bronze.
Nechosheth.

170
00:24:23,325 --> 00:24:36,459
Samson was bound in it, and some five hundred years later so was the last king of
Judah, and the Bible describes exactly those two men that way: blinded,

171
00:24:36,509 --> 00:24:39,131
then bound in bronze, then led.

172
00:24:39,181 --> 00:24:50,515
We began with the one and we end with the other. A king who had everything and would
not listen, and a judge who had everything and would not either.

173
00:24:50,565 --> 00:25:03,801
Both of them in the dark, in chains, in an enemy's city. And then the two stories
part. Zedekiah was given a promise he had not earned and died in peace.

174
00:25:03,851 --> 00:25:10,235
Samson was given his strength once more and died with his hands on the pillars.

175
00:25:10,285 --> 00:25:27,107
Six men, six words, and the chain is complete: put out, grown dim, grown heavy,
stood still, stood still, dug out. Not one of them saw again.

176
00:25:27,157 --> 00:25:34,320
And not one of them is finished in the text when his eyes are.

177
00:25:39,110 --> 00:25:51,310
What came after. The book of Judges says nothing more of him after the tomb, except
the line it had already said: he had judged Israel twenty years.

178
00:25:51,360 --> 00:26:02,340
The New Testament says one thing more, and it puts him in the same list as Isaac and
Jacob. Hebrews chapter 11, verse 32, and then 34.

179
00:26:02,890 --> 00:26:05,184
[Reader] And what more shall I say?

180
00:26:05,234 --> 00:26:18,525
[Reader] Time will not allow me to tell of Gideon, Barak, Samson, Jephthah, David,
Samuel, and the prophets, quenched the raging fire, and escaped the edge of the
sword;

181
00:26:18,575 --> 00:26:26,280
[Reader] who gained strength from weakness, became mighty in battle, and put foreign
armies to flight.

182
00:26:27,330 --> 00:26:36,702
Who gained strength from weakness. Hebrews does not say which of the names that line
belongs to, and it does not need to.

183
00:26:36,752 --> 00:26:49,960
There is one man on the list who was as weak as any other man, blind and chained in
a hall full of his enemies, and asked for strength once more, and got it.

184
00:26:50,010 --> 00:27:02,660
The same chapter that gave Isaac his one verse and Jacob his gives Samson his name,
between Barak and Jephthah, among the people who lived by faith.

185
00:27:02,710 --> 00:27:10,160
Three of the six men in this series are in that chapter. None of the three could see
when he did the thing Hebrews remembers them for.

186
00:27:14,190 --> 00:27:15,900
Brian, with the last word.

187
00:27:16,572 --> 00:27:24,797
[Brian] I think about that as I get older. Living with a disability is a bit like
dog years, seven to one, and the body spends itself faster.

188
00:27:24,847 --> 00:27:30,168
[Brian] I have to ask God all the time for the strength to do what I think His
purpose is for me.

189
00:27:30,218 --> 00:27:35,840
[Brian] Samson's prayer is the shortest one I know for that: remember me, strengthen
me, just once more.

190
00:27:38,890 --> 00:27:48,382
That is the end of the six. A king, a father and a son, two old men of one town, and
a judge.

191
00:27:48,432 --> 00:27:58,405
We stood where each of them lost his sight and faced north, from Jerusalem to
Beersheba to Goshen to Shiloh, twice, to Gaza,

192
00:27:58,455 --> 00:28:02,204
and the map ran from the Nile to the Euphrates.

193
00:28:02,254 --> 00:28:11,779
Every one of them lost his sight and lived the rest of his life without it. Not one
of them is described afterward as less than he was.

194
00:28:11,829 --> 00:28:21,880
And the Bible's one word for what three of them did in the dark is the word we end
every episode on. For we walk by faith and not by sight.

195
00:28:23,930 --> 00:28:29,272
One more thing before we go, and it is Brian's, so we will let him say it.

196
00:28:29,322 --> 00:28:38,718
But he will tell you it started with a verse, and it is the first verse this program
ever read, before any of the six,

197
00:28:38,768 --> 00:28:43,441
when the disciples asked Jesus whose fault a blind man was.

198
00:28:43,491 --> 00:28:45,640
John chapter 9, verse 3.

199
00:28:46,190 --> 00:28:54,940
[Reader] Jesus answered, “Neither this man nor his parents sinned, but this happened
so that the works of God would be displayed in him.

200
00:28:55,990 --> 00:29:07,701
The disciples asked whose fault it was. Jesus answered that it was so the works of
God would be displayed, which is to say, for His glory.

201
00:29:07,751 --> 00:29:18,980
Six men later, that is what this series has found the Bible says about blindness.
Not whose fault. Whose glory. Brian.

202
00:29:20,030 --> 00:29:28,760
[Brian] When we started this series I had a notion that blindness was the hinge of
every one of these stories, that God needed each of these men blind to make His
point.

203
00:29:28,810 --> 00:29:35,397
[Brian] Going through them one at a time, I do not think that holds all the way
across. Eli's blindness is a picture more than a cause.

204
00:29:35,447 --> 00:29:40,606
[Brian] Jacob did not need to be blind to cross his hands. But I noticed something
else instead.

205
00:29:40,656 --> 00:29:46,220
[Brian] In every one of the six, the moment that decides the story arrives with the
eyes already gone,

206
00:29:46,270 --> 00:29:53,506
[Brian] and what fills the gap is either what people say or what the Lord has said,
and the story turns on which one the man took.

207
00:29:53,556 --> 00:30:00,122
[Brian] Isaac took the people in the room and was fooled, then took the Lord's word
and blessed by faith.

208
00:30:00,172 --> 00:30:06,231
[Brian] Ahijah's eyes were beaten by a disguise, and it did not matter, because the
Lord had told him who was coming.

209
00:30:06,281 --> 00:30:11,110
[Brian] Samson, blind in a hall full of his enemies, prayed, and was heard.

210
00:30:11,160 --> 00:30:19,908
[Brian] Zedekiah's blindness was the only way two prophecies that seemed to
contradict could both come true, and it cost him more than it cost any of the
others.

211
00:30:19,958 --> 00:30:21,722
[Brian] I feel some sympathy for him.

212
00:30:21,772 --> 00:30:27,615
[Brian] The thing I keep coming back to is the first story we ever told on this
program, the man born blind,

213
00:30:27,665 --> 00:30:32,035
[Brian] where Jesus said it was so that the works of God would be displayed in him.

214
00:30:32,085 --> 00:30:40,118
[Brian] That is what I see across these six. Not that God needed anyone blind, but
that He showed something in each of them that sight would have hidden.

215
00:30:40,168 --> 00:30:48,012
[Brian] His exactness in a king. His strength in a chained man's weakness. His
knowledge in an old prophet's dark house.

216
00:30:48,062 --> 00:30:51,312
[Brian] I would be lying if I said I did not hope the same about my own.

217
00:30:51,362 --> 00:30:59,903
[Brian] Not that I was made blind for a purpose I can name, but that something of
Him can be shown in how I live without sight, in trust, in reliance,

218
00:30:59,953 --> 00:31:01,900
[Brian] in asking for strength once more.

219
00:31:03,950 --> 00:31:06,140
Next time. Brian.

220
00:31:06,690 --> 00:31:15,944
[Brian] I don't know yet. Honestly. We've gone where the stories took us, and I've
learned to be okay with not seeing the road ahead. God has led us this far.

221
00:31:15,994 --> 00:31:20,320
[Brian] He'll lead us the rest of the way. Come back next episode and we'll find out
together.

222
00:31:22,880 --> 00:31:31,483
This has been Not By Sight. The full story, with every source named, is at
blindnessinthebible.com.

223
00:31:31,533 --> 00:31:42,402
Scripture quotations are from the Holy Bible, Berean Standard Bible, which is in the
public domain. The scenes you heard marked as imagined are our own.

224
00:31:42,452 --> 00:31:49,630
Everything else is from the text and the old books. For we walk by faith, not by
sight.

